= corefs Filesystem Driver Installation

:toc: left
:icons: font
:sectlinks:

See also: link:index.html[Main Boot Page], link:bios-boot.html[BIOS Boot Process]

== Motivation

Stage2 (`core.bin`) needs filesystem access to locate and load stage3
(`libTheBootloader.so`) from the boot media. The filesystem on the boot
media (FAT32, ext2, ISO 9660, ...) is not known until image build time,
and stage2 does not want to carry every driver.

The solution is install-time composition: the build compiles each
filesystem driver into its own standalone binary (`corefs_<fs>.bin`),
linked at a fixed address, and the image builder appends the chosen
module to the boot image and patches its location into stage2 -- no
relocations, no runtime search.

== Overview

```
 Build-time (SCons)                    Image build (Python)
 +--------------------------------+    +--------------------------------+
 | boot/common/fs/fat.c           |    | stage1 boot.bin                |
 |  + disk.S (real-mode thunk)    |    | stage2 core.bin               |
 |  + mbr.c, gpt.c (partition)    |    |  + "VLSF" patched with        |
 |  -> link at 0x90000            |    |    corefs address,            |
 |  -> corefs_fat.bin             |    |    partition label, UUID      |
 |                                |    |  + corefs_<fs>.bin appended   |
 | boot/common/fs/ext2.c          |    |    (padded to land at 0x90000)|
 |  -> corefs_ext2.bin            |    |  -> bootable disk image / ISO |
 | boot/common/fs/iso9660.c       |    +--------------------------------+
 |  -> corefs_iso9660.bin         |
 +--------------------------------+
```

The image builder is `scripts/disk_builder.py` for MBR/GPT disk images
and `scripts/scons/disk.py` (`CreateBootableIso`) for El Torito ISO
images.

== Driver Export Table

Each driver exports its operations in a dedicated `.exports` section,
which the linker script (`corefs.ld`) places at the very start of the
module:

[source,ld]
----
SECTIONS
{
    . = 0x90000;

    .exports : AT(0) { *(.exports*) }
    .text    : { *(.text*) *(.rodata*) *(.data*) }
    .bss (NOLOAD) : { *(.bss*) }
}
----

The export table is a const struct of function pointers, filled in at
link time with absolute addresses (the module is linked for its final
address, so no relocations survive):

[source,c]
----
static const FatOperations fs_exports
    __attribute__((section(".exports"), used)) = {
        .Initialize = corefs_Initialize,
        .Open = corefs_Open,
        .Read = corefs_Read,
        .Close = corefs_Close,
        .DISK_Read = DISK_Read,
        .DISK_ReadLBA = DISK_ReadLBA,
};
----

All three drivers (FAT, ext2, ISO 9660) export the same six-member
operations struct:

[source,c]
----
struct FatOperations
{
   int (*Initialize)(const uint8_t *bios_drive_list, uint32_t count,
                     const uint8_t *partition_uuid,
                     const uint8_t *partition_label);
   int (*Open)(const char *path);
   int (*Read)(int fd, void *buffer, int count);
   int (*Close)(int fd);
   int (*DISK_Read)(uint8_t drive, uint16_t cylinder, uint8_t sector,
                    uint8_t head, uint8_t count, void *buffer);
   int (*DISK_ReadLBA)(uint8_t drive, uint64_t lba, uint16_t count,
                       void *buffer);
};
----

The `DISK_*` members come from `disk.S`: a self-contained 16-bit thunk
(own GDT, real-mode switch, INT 13h call, switch back) shared by all
corefs modules. Stage2 calls the driver through the pointer stored in
`boot_params.corefs_ops`, so it never calls `DISK_*` directly.

== Install-Time Patching

Stage2 contains a small patch region identified by the "VLSF"
signature. The image builder overwrites the fields after it:

```
"VLSF"  corefs load address (u32)   partition label (32 bytes)   partition UUID (16 bytes)
  0             4                           8                             40
```

* `corefs load address` -- where the module was appended (`0x90000`); stage2 stores it in `boot_params.corefs_ops`
* `partition label` / `partition UUID` -- the label/UUID of the boot partition, used by the driver to find its partition; for disk images they come from `blkid` after mkfs, for ISO images they are read back from the ISO Primary Volume Descriptor

The module itself is appended right after stage2 and padded with zeros
so that it starts exactly at its link address (`0x90000`), making the
absolute addresses in its export table valid. This is done by
`BuildStage2Blob()` in `scripts/disk_builder.py` and `CreateElTorito()`
in `scripts/scons/bootloader.py`.

== Runtime Flow

```
stage2 entry (real mode, 0x7E00)
  |
  +-- detect_hardware()        -- Multiboot2 info at 0x6000, drive list at 0x5100
  |
  +-- A20, GDT, protected mode -- stack at 0x9FC00
  |
  +-- build boot_params        -- mbi tags, corefs_ops ("VLSF" address),
  |                              drive list, partition label/UUID
  |
  +-- main()
        |
        +-- corefs->Initialize(drive_list, count, uuid, label)
        |     +-- probe partition tables (MBR/GPT) on each drive
        |     +-- find partition matching label/UUID, mount it
        |
        +-- corefs->Open("/boot/libTheBootloader-<ver>_<config>.so")
        +-- corefs->Read(...)   -- into stage3_buf (512 KB)
        +-- corefs->Close(...)
        |
        +-- patch "VLSO" signature with the DL callback struct
        +-- DL_LoadLibrary(...) -- resolve ELF relocations in place
        +-- parse /boot/TheBootloader.conf, draw boot menu
```

The driver never touches hardware directly: all disk I/O goes through
`DISK_Read` / `DISK_ReadLBA`, which are provided by the shared
`disk.S` real-mode thunk. The partition probing (`mbr.c`, `gpt.c`) is
also shared between the modules.

== Layout and Constraints

[width="100%",cols="3,1,4"]
|===
|Artifact |Link address |Description

|`boot.bin`
|`0x7C00`
|Stage1 MBR bootsector.

|`core.bin`
|`0x7E00`
|Stage2: real-mode detection, video setup, corefs init, stage3 dynamic loading. Contains the "VLSF" patch region.

|`corefs_fat.bin` / `corefs_ext2.bin` / `corefs_iso9660.bin`
|`0x90000`
|Read-only filesystem driver: partition probing, directory traversal, file reads. One module per image.

|`stage3_buf`
|`0xE900` (512 KB)
|Buffer that receives `libTheBootloader.so` before it is dynamically loaded.

|Stage2 stack
|`0x9FC00`
|Grows down from just below the 640 KB mark.
|===

Constraints:

* All corefs modules are linked at the same address (`0x90000`) because only one is ever present in a given boot image.
* Stage2 (including its 512 KB stage3 buffer) must fit between `0x7E00` and `0x90000` (~545 KB).
* The corefs module must fit between `0x90000` and the stack at `0x9FC00` (roughly 63 KB; current modules are about 8-12 KB).
* The driver runs with real-mode BIOS services available (via the `disk.S` thunk) because `DISK_Read` ultimately issues INT 13h.

== Alternatives Considered

=== Partial linking at install time
Linking `core.o` together with the chosen driver at image build time.
Rejected because it requires the cross-linker on the machine that
builds the image.

=== Loading the driver from raw disk sectors at runtime
Storing the driver in dedicated sectors next to stage2 and reading it
at runtime (like stage1 loads stage2). Rejected because it adds boot
media layout complexity for no gain: the driver fits comfortably inside
the boot image.

=== Function-address patch table ("VLPT") + Python bundler
An earlier design where core_common.bin carried a patch table of
function addresses ("VLPT") and a `bundle_core.py` script copied the
driver's entry points into it. Superseded by the current approach, which
appends the whole module at its link address and patches only its base
address, the partition label and the partition UUID into stage2.

== Revision History

=== v2.0

Rewritten to match the implemented corefs mechanism: whole-module
append at `0x90000` with "VLSF" patching (address, label, UUID) instead
of the earlier "VLPT" function-address patch table and `bundle_core.py`.
The old design is kept under Alternatives Considered.

=== v1.0

Initial documentation of the install-time FS driver composition mechanism.
